          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-tenant-of-wildfell-hall-f2fa9760d2985024\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"The rhetorical question “Do I love you!” functions as an emphatic affirmative avowal: in context, it means roughly “Of course I love you.” It presents his love as true but does not itself undertake a commitment. Although delivered passionately, there is no independent evidence that the words are a reflex-like expressive discharge rather than an intense assertion.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Its interrogative form could superficially suggest a request for confirmation or express incredulity at Helen’s doubt, but the ensuing protestation of the truth and fervour of his attachment strongly fixes its primary force as an emphatic avowal.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A directly represented spoken response by Helen’s interlocutor, embedded as dialogue within her first-person diary narrative.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Helen asks whether he really loves her as part of an effort to test whether his professed love is sufficiently disinterested for him to respect her wishes. His rhetorical reply emphatically affirms the attachment, and the subsequent protestation explicitly concerns its truth and fervour. His broader speech is pressuring and self-serving, while Helen doubts the moral quality of his love; those contextual complications bear on sincerity, selfishness, and manipulation, not on the core utterance’s predominantly truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Helen asks, “Do you really love me?” and he replies, “Do I love you!”\",\"supports\":\"The reply echoes her question rhetorically and functions as a strongly affirmative avowal rather than a genuine information-seeking question.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately afterward, “He commenced a passionate protestation of the truth and fervour of his attachment.”\",\"supports\":\"The narration explicitly interprets the response and continuation as asserting that his attachment is true and fervent, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The speaker has already said that for more than three years he has suffered from “suppressed emotions,” “longings,” and “trampled affections.”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding speech frames the utterance as an avowal of an enduring emotional state.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Helen follows with “Truly?” and then asks whether his love is selfish and whether it can motivate sacrifice for her welfare.\",\"supports\":\"Her response treats his utterance as a claim of love whose truth and quality are being tested, rather than as a commitment enacted by the words themselves.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the rhetorical avowal. Its passionate delivery and surrounding manipulation can be described contextually without adding E, P, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
